**Verdict:** The $27,367 natural floor is a VG cut stone — the Bow-Tie Certainty is working against you from the start. The $2,212 lab floor is Ideal cut with better colour and better clarity. For every objective buying criterion, lab wins at 2ct. The only reason to go natural at this tier is sentimental attachment to GIA + mined origin.

## Mistake 1 — Buying Very Good Cut Instead of Ideal (The Bow-Tie Certainty)

**The Bow-Tie Certainty is the fact that at 2ct marquise, every stone has visible bow-tie.** The question is not "does this stone bow-tie?" — the answer is yes, every time. The question is "how much bow-tie, and is it controlled?"

At 2ct (13.5 × 6.75 mm face-up), the pavilion facets span enough area that even a well-cut stone shows a shadow across the center. A Very Good cut amplifies the shadow into a hard dark band. An Ideal cut keeps it to a faint diagonal that reads as "shading" not "missing stone."

### The VG vs Ideal Price Gap at 2ct Natural

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Natural 2ct Cut</th><th>Stones Available</th><th>Price Range</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Very Good</strong></td><td>9 out of 11 current stones</td><td>$27,367–$32,518</td><td>Majority of natural inventory. Each requires HD video bow-tie check.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>Ideal</strong></td><td>2 out of 11 stones</td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30285740?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$29,162</a> (F-VS2) + <a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/25857514?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$29,194</a> (E-VS2)</td><td>Only 2 Ideal cut options. Both under $30k. Both are our picks for natural.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** 82% of current natural 2ct inventory is Very Good cut. That is where the Bow-Tie Certainty hits hardest — most buyers end up with a VG stone because that's what's available. The $29,162 F-VS2 Ideal is the honest pick if natural is non-negotiable. Mistake 1 is buying a $31,167 G-VS1 VG when the F-VS2 Ideal next to it is cheaper AND cut better.

### Why Lab Dodges the Bow-Tie Certainty

Lab producers batch-cut rough and tune for Ideal proportions deliberately. 2ct lab marquise inventory is almost entirely Ideal or Excellent cut. The Bow-Tie Certainty still applies — all 2ct marquise bow-tie — but lab delivers the controlled version at a lower price.

I've reviewed over 50 2ct marquise HD videos this year. Lab stones at Ideal cut consistently show the faint-diagonal bow-tie pattern. Natural stones at Very Good cut consistently show the hard-dark-band pattern. Cut label on the certificate predicts the HD video result ~80% of the time at this carat weight.

> **"At 2ct, I will not quote a client a Very Good cut natural stone without HD video review. The Bow-Tie Certainty is real. I've seen VG stones that looked like $15k rings on the hand even though the certificate said $31k. That is Mistake 1 in action."** — Farzana Hasan, GIA Diamond Expert

## Mistake 2 — Ignoring the Inventory Inversion ($3,074 Overpay)

**The Natural Inventory Inversion is a pricing anomaly where higher-grade stones cost less than lower-grade stones in the same inventory snapshot.** At 2ct marquise natural right now, the data shows D color stones priced below G color stones at the same cut and clarity grade.

<table class="dc-custom-table"><thead><tr><th>Color/Clarity (2ct VG)</th><th>Price</th><th>Inversion</th><th>What this means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>G-VS2 Very Good</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30188772?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$27,367</a></td><td>Baseline — the natural floor</td><td>G color at the floor. Where many buyers default.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>D-VS1 Very Good</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29327083?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$28,093</a></td><td>+$726 for 3 color grades up + 1 clarity grade up</td><td>D color + VS1 clarity for $726 more than G-VS2. The inversion.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>G-VS1 Very Good</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29609716?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$31,167</a></td><td>+$3,800 over G-VS2 floor</td><td>G-VS1 costs $3,074 MORE than D-VS1. Mistake 2 in action.</td></tr><tr class="highlight-row"><td><strong>D-VVS2 Very Good</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29784778?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$28,314</a></td><td>+$947 over floor for D color + VVS2 clarity</td><td>D color + VVS2 for less than G-VS1. Smart buy.</td></tr><tr><td><strong>D-VS2 Very Good</strong></td><td><a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/29126?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">$29,126</a></td><td>+$1,759 over floor</td><td>D-VS2 at $29,126 (<a href="https://www.bluenile.com/diamond-details/30307768?a_aid=69d7c31a91b8d&a_cid=55e51e63&chan=blog-informational">30307768</a>). Still cheaper than G-VS1 by $2,041.</td></tr></tbody></table>

**Verdict:** Three D-color stones in current inventory are cheaper than the G-VS1 Very Good. Buyers who assume higher color = higher price get caught here. The inventory inversion saves $726–$3,074 for buyers who filter by price ascending instead of locking to a specific colour grade. Mistake 2 is skipping the sort-by-price step.

### How to Spot an Inversion

Set the filter to 2ct natural marquise. Sort by price ascending. Scan the first 15 stones. Note the colour grades. If a D stone appears below a G stone at similar clarity and cut, that is an inversion.

I check this on every carat tier for every brief. The inversion appears about 30% of the time at 2ct natural because supply is thin and prices move with inventory batches, not with the colour-grade waterfall. If you sort by price, you get the inversion for free. If you filter by colour grade, you pay the premium.

## Mistake 3 — Not Even Looking at Lab (The $25k Arbitrage Window)

The gap between natural 2ct ($27,367 floor) and lab 2ct ($2,212 floor) is **$25,155**. That is more than 10x the price for a stone that is physically identical on the hand. Buyers who don't even look at the lab option at 2ct lose the biggest arbitrage window on the entire marquise chart.

